::heading level=1 id=welcome-title
Welcome to dx
::end

::paragraph id=welcome-lead
A .dx file is a sheet of paper you can run. It holds typed blocks — prose, tables, drawings, code — so the same document parses deterministically, renders identically in an editor, a browser, and an agent's eyes, and keeps whatever its code produced.
::end

::heading level=2 id=what-this-example-shows
What this example shows
::end

::html id=welcome-kpi-grid
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Status</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td>Typed blocks</td><td>Yes</td><td>No grammar ambiguity for parser or tooling.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Tables and drawings</td><td>Yes</td><td>A chart is document content, not an attachment.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Content-addressed store</td><td>Yes</td><td>Every read resolves a pointer to the true document.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>One renderer</td><td>Yes</td><td>Editor, CLI, and agent see the same bytes.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
::end

